course: physics
lesson: 37
title: Seat belts, crashes and keeping safe
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: In a crash the car stops in a moment, but everything inside it, you, your bag and the dog, keeps moving forward at the speed the car had.
explain: A seat belt stops you together with the car and spreads the force over the strong bones of your shoulder and hips, which is why it saves lives.
explain: Cars are built to soften the stop: the front crumples slowly, an airbag puffs out in a split second, and a longer, softer stop means a smaller force on the people inside.
explain: Nothing heavy can stop instantly: a car needs time and distance, and a heavy train needs over a kilometre, so the safest plan is never to be in the way.
explain: Being seen is the first safety and being protected is the second, for a cyclist in bright clothes and a helmet just as for a child near a lorry's blind spot.
fact: In a crash the car stops suddenly but the people inside keep moving forward, and a seat belt stops them together with the car.
fact: A seat belt spreads the force over strong bones, the shoulder and hips, not the soft tummy or neck.
fact: A seat belt locks when tugged sharply, because a little weight inside swings and locks the reel, but slides out when pulled slowly.
fact: Younger children use booster seats so the adult belt crosses their shoulder and hips instead of their neck and tummy.
fact: An airbag fills with gas in a split second and slows your head more gently than the hard wheel; small children must not sit in front of an airbag.
fact: A car's front crumple zone squashes in a crash and makes the stop take a little longer, so the force on the people is smaller, while the strong cabin keeps its shape.
fact: A loose bag on a seat keeps moving in a crash and becomes a flying object; heavy things go in the boot and pets ride in a carrier or harness.
fact: A cyclist's helmet softens a knock to the head, and bright clothes and lights help drivers see the cyclist.
fact: It is a myth that a car can stop instantly: even the best brakes need time and distance.
fact: Big vehicles have blind spots; if you cannot see the driver's face in the mirror, the driver cannot see you.
fact: A fast train can need over a kilometre to stop, because it is very heavy and steel wheels on steel rails have little grip; that is why level crossings have barriers.
exercise: Why does a seat belt save lives in a crash? | It stops you together with the car instead of letting you fly forward | It makes the car stop more slowly | It keeps you warm during the crash | It pulls you towards the back seat
exercise: Which parts of the body should a seat belt cross? | The shoulder and the hips | The neck and the soft tummy | The arms and the tops of the legs | The head and the middle of the chest
exercise: Why can't you pull a seat belt out fast? | A little weight inside swings and locks the reel when it is jerked | The belt is glued until the engine starts | The belt is too short to pull quickly | The belt only moves when the car moves
exercise: Why can you lean forward slowly in a seat belt but not suddenly? | A sudden jerk is a sign of a crash, so the belt locks | Slow movements are too weak to feel | The belt is made of stretchy rubber that only gives slowly | The seat moves with you slowly
exercise: Why do younger children need booster seats? | So the adult belt fits across their shoulder and hips | So they can see out of the window | So they are closer to the airbag and get its full protection | To keep them from getting bored
exercise: What does an airbag do in a crash? | Fills with gas in a split second and slows your head gently | Pushes the car away from the crash | Holds the steering wheel still | Opens the doors so you can get out
exercise: Why do small children sit in the back, not in front of an airbag? | An airbag hits very hard and can hurt a small child | The back seats are warmer and comfier for small children | Airbags only work for children | Children are heavier than adults
exercise: Why are cars made to crumple at the front? | A longer stop means a smaller force on the people inside | Crumpled metal is easier to recycle once the car is scrapped | It makes the car lighter after a crash | It hides the engine from view
exercise: What should stay strong and keep its shape in a crash? | The cabin where the people sit | The front bumper and the grille | The bonnet over the top of the engine | The headlights and the wing mirrors
exercise: Why should heavy things go in the boot? | A loose heavy thing becomes a flying object in a crash | The boot keeps heavy things cooler | Heavy things make the seats wear out and sag much faster | The car drives faster that way
exercise: What are the two main ways a cyclist stays safe? | Being seen, and being protected by a helmet | Riding fast and riding at night when the roads are empty | Riding without lights to save battery | Ringing the bell all the time
exercise: Why should you never step out in front of a braking car? | A car needs time and distance to stop | Brakes make cars go faster at first | Drivers always swerve towards you | Cars can stop instantly, but drivers forget
exercise: How can you tell if a lorry driver can see you? | If you can see the driver's face in the mirror | If you can hear the engine running close beside you | If the lorry is moving slowly and has its lights on | If you wave both arms high above your head at him
exercise: Why can't a train stop quickly for someone on the track? | It is very heavy and steel wheels on rails have little grip | The driver is not allowed to brake until the train reaches a station | Trains have no brakes at all | The track pulls the train along
exercise: Why do level crossings have barriers? | A fast train can need over a kilometre to stop | Trains like to be watched from behind barriers | The barriers make trains go faster | Cars are not allowed near railways at all
test: nah i don't need a belt, i'll just hold onto the seat if we crash. right? | No, in a crash your body keeps going far harder than your arms can hold | Yes, holding on is just as safe | Yes, if you hold tightly with both hands | No, you should hold the door handle instead
test: A pet dog is loose on the back seat. What could happen in a crash? | It would keep moving forward and fly through the car | It would stay still on the seat, because it is so light | It would be pushed into the back window | Nothing, dogs always land on their feet
test: In a cartoon, a car smashes into a wall and the front stays perfect while the people are thrown about. What's wrong? | Real cars crumple at the front to protect the people | Cars never crumple in crashes | The wall should be the thing that crumples, not the car | People in cars never move in a crash
test: Max is seven and small. Dad says he can use the adult belt without a booster. What's the problem? | The belt would cross his neck and tummy instead of shoulder and hips | Adult belts are too short for children | Boosters only matter on long journeys | Small children slip under the airbag
test: mate says cars can stop dead the second the driver hits the brakes. true? | No, a car always needs time and distance to stop | Yes, modern brakes work instantly the moment they are pressed | Yes, but only on dry roads with good brand-new tyres | Yes, if the driver presses the pedal really hard
test: You are on your bike next to a big lorry at the lights and cannot see the driver in his mirror. What's true? | The driver cannot see you, so move where he can | He can always see you through the window in the side door | He can always see cyclists | It does not matter at the lights
test: Why are the strong bones of the hips and shoulder the best place for a belt to press? | They can take the force without serious harm | They are the softest and most cushioned parts of the body | They are the lightest parts of the body, so they move less | They are the parts that sit closest to the car floor
test: Why does an airbag make a crash safer than hitting the steering wheel? | It slows the head over a longer, softer stop | It bounces you straight back into your seat again | It makes the whole car stop instantly where it is | It pushes the other car away before you hit it
test: A heavy suitcase sits on the back seat next to your sister. What should Dad do? | Put it in the boot | Put it on her lap | Tie it to the headrest with a ribbon | Leave it, it's heavy so it won't move
test: Why should you never cross a railway when the barriers are down, even if the train looks far away? | Trains are fast and need a very long way to stop | Trains can steer round people | The barriers are only for cars, so walkers can go round them | Trains always stop for people
test: Lily cycles home at dusk in a dark coat with no lights. What's the biggest problem? | Drivers may not see her | She will get cold | Her bike will go slower | The helmet will not work in the dark
exam: Identify the principal function of the crumple zone of a motor vehicle. | To prolong the duration of the collision and so reduce the force upon the occupants | To render the vehicle lighter subsequent to a collision | To protect the engine at the expense of the occupants | To arrest the vehicle instantaneously upon impact
exam: Ascertain the reason for which a child of small stature is required to employ a booster seat. | So that the belt passes across the shoulder and hips rather than the neck and abdomen | So that the child may observe the road more readily | So that the child is positioned nearer to the airbag | So that the child's weight is increased for safety
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the behaviour of unsecured objects within a vehicle at the moment of collision? | They continue to move forward at the former speed of the vehicle | They remain at rest in their original positions | They are propelled towards the rear of the vehicle by the impact | They are rendered weightless by the impact
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the visibility of pedestrians to the driver of a large goods vehicle. | If the driver's face cannot be seen in the mirror, the driver cannot see the pedestrian | The driver can at all times see any pedestrian adjacent to the cab | Large vehicles possess no areas concealed from the driver | Pedestrians are visible to the driver only when the vehicle is stationary
